[38;2;253;151;31mCONFIGURATION FILE[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       The Git configuration file contains a number of variables that affect the Git commands' behavior. The files .git/config and optionally config.worktree (see the "CONFIGURATION FILE" section of [0m[38;2;166;226;46mgit-worktree[0m[38;2;249;38;114m([0m[38;2;190;132;255m1[0m[38;2;249;38;114m)[0m[38;2;248;248;242m) in each repository[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       are used to store the configuration for that repository, and [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mHOME[0m[38;2;248;248;242m/.gitconfig is used to store a per-user configuration as fallback values for the .git/config file. The file /etc/gitconfig can be used to store a system-wide[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       default configuration.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       The configuration variables are used by both the Git plumbing and the porcelain commands. The variables are divided into sections, wherein the fully qualified variable name of the variable itself is the last dot-separated[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       segment and the section name is everything before the last dot. The variable names are case-insensitive, allow only alphanumeric characters and -, and must start with an alphabetic character. Some variables may appear multiple[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       times; we say then that the variable is multivalued.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Syntax[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       The syntax is fairly flexible and permissive. Whitespace characters, which in this context are the space character (SP) and the horizontal tabulation (HT), are mostly ignored. The # and ; characters begin comments to the end of[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       line. Blank lines are ignored.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       The file consists of sections and variables. A section begins with the name of the section in square brackets and continues until the next section begins. Section names are case-insensitive. Only alphanumeric characters, - and .[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       are allowed in section names. Each variable must belong to some section, which means that there must be a section header before the first setting of a variable.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       Sections can be further divided into subsections. To begin a subsection put its name in double quotes, separated by space from the section name, in the section header, like in the example below:[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m                   [section "subsection"][0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       Subsection names are case sensitive and can contain any characters except newline and the null byte. Doublequote " and backslash can be included by escaping them as \" and \\, respectively. Backslashes preceding other characters[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       are dropped when reading; for example, \t is read as t and \0 is read as 0. Section headers cannot span multiple lines. Variables may belong directly to a section or to a given subsection. You can have [section] if you have[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       [section "subsection"], but you don’t need to.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       There is also a deprecated [section.subsection] syntax. With this syntax, the subsection name is converted to lower-case and is also compared case sensitively. These subsection names follow the same restrictions as section[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       names.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       All the other lines (and the remainder of the line after the section header) are recognized as setting variables, in the form name = value (or just name, which is a short-hand to say that the variable is the boolean "true"). The[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       variable names are case-insensitive, allow only alphanumeric characters and -, and must start with an alphabetic character.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       Whitespace characters surrounding name, = and value are discarded. Internal whitespace characters within value are retained verbatim. Comments starting with either # or ; and extending to the end of line are discarded. A line[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       that defines a value can be continued to the next line by ending it with a backslash (\); the backslash and the end-of-line characters are discarded.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       If value needs to contain leading or trailing whitespace characters, it must be enclosed in double quotation marks ("). Inside double quotation marks, double quote (") and backslash (\) characters must be escaped: use \" for "[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       and \\ for \.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       The following escape sequences (beside \" and \\) are recognized: \n for newline character (NL), \t for horizontal tabulation (HT, TAB) and \b for backspace (BS). Other char escape sequences (including octal escape sequences)[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       are invalid.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Includes[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       The include and includeIf sections allow you to include config directives from another source. These sections behave identically to each other with the exception that includeIf sections may be ignored if their condition does not[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       evaluate to true; see "Conditional includes" below.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       You can include a config file from another by setting the special include.path (or includeIf.*.path) variable to the name of the file to be included. The variable takes a pathname as its value, and is subject to tilde expansion.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       These variables can be given multiple times.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       The contents of the included file are inserted immediately, as if they had been found at the location of the include directive. If the value of the variable is a relative path, the path is considered to be relative to the[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       configuration file in which the include directive was found. See below for examples.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Conditional includes[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       You can conditionally include a config file from another by setting an includeIf.<condition>.path variable to the name of the file to be included.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       The condition starts with a keyword followed by a colon and some data whose format and meaning depends on the keyword. Supported keywords are:[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       gitdir[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           The data that follows the keyword gitdir and a colon is used as a glob pattern. If the location of the .git directory matches the pattern, the include condition is met.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The .git location may be auto-discovered, or come from [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mGIT_DIR[0m[38;2;248;248;242m environment variable. If the repository is auto-discovered via a .git file (e.g. from submodules, or a linked worktree), the .git location would be the final[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           location where the .git directory is, not where the .git file is.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The pattern can contain standard globbing wildcards and two additional ones, **/ and /**, that can match multiple path components. Please refer to [0m[38;2;166;226;46mgitignore[0m[38;2;249;38;114m([0m[38;2;190;132;255m5[0m[38;2;249;38;114m)[0m[38;2;248;248;242m for details. For convenience:[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           •   If the pattern starts with ~/, ~ will be substituted with the content of the environment variable HOME.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           •   If the pattern starts with ./, it is replaced with the directory containing the current config file.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           •   If the pattern does not start with either ~/, ./ or /, **/ will be automatically prepended. For example, the pattern foo/bar becomes **/foo/bar and would match /any/path/to/foo/bar.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           •   If the pattern ends with /, ** will be automatically added. For example, the pattern foo/ becomes foo/**. In other words, it matches "foo" and everything inside, recursively.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       gitdir/i[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           This is the same as gitdir except that matching is done case-insensitively (e.g. on case-insensitive file systems)[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       onbranch[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           The data that follows the keyword onbranch and a colon is taken to be a pattern with standard globbing wildcards and two additional ones, **/ and /**, that can match multiple path components. If we are in a worktree where[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           the name of the branch that is currently checked out matches the pattern, the include condition is met.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           If the pattern ends with /, ** will be automatically added. For example, the pattern foo/ becomes foo/**. In other words, it matches all branches that begin with foo/. This is useful if your branches are organized[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           hierarchically and you would like to apply a configuration to all the branches in that hierarchy.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       hasconfig:remote.*.url[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           The data that follows this keyword and a colon is taken to be a pattern with standard globbing wildcards and two additional ones, **/ and /**, that can match multiple components. The first time this keyword is seen, the rest[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           of the config files will be scanned for remote URLs (without applying any values). If there exists at least one remote URL that matches this pattern, the include condition is met.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           Files included by this option (directly or indirectly) are not allowed to contain remote URLs.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           Note that unlike other includeIf conditions, resolving this condition relies on information that is not yet known at the point of reading the condition. A typical use case is this option being present as a system-level or[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           global-level config, and the remote URL being in a local-level config; hence the need to scan ahead when resolving this condition. In order to avoid the chicken-and-egg problem in which potentially-included files can affect[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           whether such files are potentially included, Git breaks the cycle by prohibiting these files from affecting the resolution of these conditions (thus, prohibiting them from declaring remote URLs).[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           As for the naming of this keyword, it is for forwards compatibility with a naming scheme that supports more variable-based include conditions, but currently Git only supports the exact keyword described above.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       A few more notes on matching via gitdir and gitdir/i:[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       •   Symlinks in [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mGIT_DIR[0m[38;2;248;248;242m are not resolved before matching.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       •   Both the symlink & realpath versions of paths will be matched outside of [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mGIT_DIR[0m[38;2;248;248;242m. E.g. if ~/git is a symlink to /mnt/storage/git, both gitdir:~/git and gitdir:/mnt/storage/git will match.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           This was not the case in the initial release of this feature in v2.13.0, which only matched the realpath version. Configuration that wants to be compatible with the initial release of this feature needs to either specify[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           only the realpath version, or both versions.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       •   Note that "../" is not special and will match literally, which is unlikely what you want.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Example[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           # Core variables[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [core][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   ; Don't trust file modes[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   filemode = false[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           # Our diff algorithm[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [diff][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   external = /usr/local/bin/diff-wrapper[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   renames = true[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           [branch "devel"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   remote = origin[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   merge = refs/heads/devel[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           # Proxy settings[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [core][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   gitProxy="ssh" for "kernel.org"[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   gitProxy=default-proxy ; for the rest[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           [include][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = /path/to/foo.inc ; include by absolute path[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = foo.inc ; find "foo.inc" relative to the current file[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = ~/foo.inc ; find "foo.inc" in your ‘[0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mHOME[0m[38;2;248;248;242m‘ directory[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; include if [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mGIT_DIR[0m[38;2;248;248;242m is /path/to/foo/.git[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "gitdir:/path/to/foo/.git"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = /path/to/foo.inc[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; include for all repositories inside /path/to/group[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "gitdir:/path/to/group/"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = /path/to/foo.inc[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; include for all repositories inside [0m[38;2;255;255;255m$[0m[38;2;190;132;255mHOME[0m[38;2;248;248;242m/to/group[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "gitdir:~/to/group/"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = /path/to/foo.inc[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; relative paths are always relative to the including[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           ; file (if the condition is true); their location is not[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           ; affected by the condition[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "gitdir:/path/to/group/"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = foo.inc[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; include only if we are in a worktree where foo-branch is[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           ; currently checked out[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "onbranch:foo-branch"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = foo.inc[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           ; include only if a remote with the given URL exists (note[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           ; that such a URL may be provided later in a file or in a[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           ; file read after this file is read, as seen in this example)[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [includeIf "hasconfig:remote.*.url:https://example.com/**"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   path = foo.inc[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           [remote "origin"][0m
[38;2;248;248;242m                   url = https://example.com/git[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Values[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       Values of many variables are treated as a simple string, but there are variables that take values of specific types and there are rules as to how to spell them.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       boolean[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           When a variable is said to take a boolean value, many synonyms are accepted for true and false; these are all case-insensitive.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           true[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m               Boolean true literals are yes, on, true, and 1. Also, a variable defined without = <value> is taken as true.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           false[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m               Boolean false literals are no, off, false, 0 and the empty string.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m               When converting a value to its canonical form using the [0m[38;2;166;226;46m--type[0m[38;2;249;38;114m=[0m[38;2;253;151;31mbool[0m[38;2;248;248;242m type specifier, git config will ensure that the output is "true" or "false" (spelled in lowercase).[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       integer[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           The value for many variables that specify various sizes can be suffixed with k, M,... to mean "scale the number by 1024", "by 1024x1024", etc.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       color[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           The value for a variable that takes a color is a list of colors (at most two, one for foreground and one for background) and attributes (as many as you want), separated by spaces.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The basic colors accepted are normal, black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, white and default. The first color given is the foreground; the second is the background. All the basic colors except normal and default[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           have a bright variant that can be specified by prefixing the color with bright, like brightred.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The color normal makes no change to the color. It is the same as an empty string, but can be used as the foreground color when specifying a background color alone (for example, "normal red").[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The color default explicitly resets the color to the terminal default, for example to specify a cleared background. Although it varies between terminals, this is usually not the same as setting to "white black".[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           Colors may also be given as numbers between 0 and 255; these use ANSI 256-color mode (but note that not all terminals may support this). If your terminal supports it, you may also specify 24-bit RGB values as hex, like[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           #ff0ab3, or 12-bit RGB values like #f1b, which is equivalent to the 24-bit color #ff11bb.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The accepted attributes are bold, dim, ul, blink, reverse, italic, and strike (for crossed-out or "strikethrough" letters). The position of any attributes with respect to the colors (before, after, or in between), doesn’t[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m           matter. Specific attributes may be turned off by prefixing them with no or no- (e.g., noreverse, no-ul, etc).[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m           The pseudo-attribute reset resets all colors and attributes before applying the specified coloring. For example, reset green will result in a green foreground and default background without any active attributes.[0m

